{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_vprqsphz3c7dtohbnyhot45zwh6ko2s6sc3wr7n4rxj3udndbika","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Fkf11ZHqqXH143gDdzweMU","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write regexp differential tests","prompt":"Working directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hWAEAP/translated_rust\n\nTask: write ONE new file `tests/regexp.rs` containing differential tests for the MuJS regexp\nengine. Do NOT modify any other file (especially not anything under `c_src/`, not\n`tests/common/mod.rs`, not `CONFIGS.md`/`ERRORS.md`/`SYMBOLS.md`).\n\n## Context you must read first\n1. `tests/common/mod.rs` — the harness. It exposes:\n   - `mod common; use common::*;`\n   - `Api` with every exported symbol as a field of function-pointer type. Relevant ones:\n     ```\n     js_regcomp:  unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(*const c_char, c_int, *mut *const c_char) -> *mut c_void\n     js_regcompx: unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(Alloc, *mut c_void, *const c_char, c_int, *mut *const c_char) -> *mut c_void\n     js_regexec:  unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(*mut c_void, *const c_char, *mut Resub, c_int) -> c_int\n     js_regfree:  unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(*mut c_void)\n     js_regfreex: unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(Alloc, *mut c_void, *mut c_void)\n     ```\n   - `pub fn both<T>(f: impl FnMut(&Api, Side) -> T) -> (T, T)` — runs the closure against the\n     C library then the Rust library and returns both results. THIS is how you compare.\n   - `Resub` / `ResubSpan` (`#[repr(C)]`, `Resub::default()`), `REG_MAXSUB = 16`,\n     `REG_ICASE = 1`, `REG_NEWLINE = 2`, `REG_NOTBOL = 4`.\n   - `Alloc = Option<unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, *mut c_void, c_int) -> *mut c_void>`\n   - `cs(&str) -> CString`, `cstr_string(*const c_char) -> Option<String>`,\n     `Rng::new(seed)` with `next_u32/below/range/pick`.\n   - `tests/utf.rs` is a working example of the style — read it.\n2. `c_src/src/regexp.c` and `c_src/src/regexp.h` — the ground truth. Read `regexec`\n   and `regcompx` signatures/semantics carefully.\n\n## IMPORTANT comparison rule\n`Reprog*` pointers and the `sp`/`ep` pointers inside `Resub` are raw addresses and will\nNEVER be equal between the two libraries. You must compare only *derived, address-independent*\nvalues:\n- for `regcomp`: whether the result was NULL, and the error string via `cstr_string`.\n- for `regexec`: the `int` return value, `sub.nsub`, and for each of the 16 slots either\n  `None` (sp is NULL) or `Some((sp - subject_base, ep - subject_base))` as `isize` offsets,\n  plus the matched substring bytes.\nWrite one helper `fn exec_result(api:&Api, prog:*mut c_void, subject:&CString, sub:Option<&mut Resub>, eflags:c_int) -> (c_int, c_int, Vec<Option<(isize,isize,Vec<u8>)>>)`\nand use it everywhere.\n\n## Tests to write (one `#[test]` fn per group, named as shown)\n\n`b1_b6_cflags_eflags_cross_product`: for a list of ~60 hand-written patterns covering\nliterals, `.`, `^`, `$`, `\\b`, `\\B`, classes, quantifiers, alternation, groups, lookahead,\nback-references — compile with EVERY cflags value in\n`[0, REG_ICASE, REG_NEWLINE, REG_ICASE|REG_NEWLINE, 8, 16, -1, 0x7fffffff]` and exec against\n~25 subjects (empty, ASCII, mixed case, multi-line with `\\n`, UTF-8, 200 chars) with every\neflags in `[0, REG_NOTBOL, 8, -1]`. Assert compile result and exec result match.\n(This single test covers CONFIGS rows B1-B6, B9-B13, B17.)\n\n`b7_regexec_null_sub`: same patterns/subjects but pass `sub = NULL`; assert return values match.\n\n`b8_nsub_handling`: patterns with 0..17 capture groups; set `sub.nsub` to a wrong value (0, 1,\n99) before calling and assert the post-call `nsub` and all slots match between C and Rust.\n\n`b14_empty_pattern`: `\"\"` with all 4 cflags, exec against several subjects.\n\n`b15_null_errorp`: `regcomp(pattern, cflags, std::ptr::null_mut())` for a valid and an invalid\npattern; assert NULL-ness of the result matches and neither crashes.\n\n`b16_regcompx_custom_allocator`: define a counting allocator\n```\nstatic mut ALLOC_CALLS: c_int = 0;\nunsafe extern \"C\" fn counting_alloc(_ctx:*mut c_void, ptr:*mut c_void, n:c_int)->*mut c_void {\n    ALLOC_CALLS += 1;\n    if n == 0 { libc-free equivalent; return null } else { realloc }\n}\n```\nUse `std::alloc` or link to libc via `extern \"C\" { fn realloc(...); fn free(...); }` —\ndo NOT add a dependency on the `libc` crate (it is only a normal dependency, so it IS\navailable to tests; you may use `libc::realloc`/`libc::free`). Compile a set of patterns with\n`js_regcompx` + the allocator, exec them, free with `js_regfreex`, and assert results equal\n`js_regcomp`/`js_regfree` results AND that the allocation-call count is identical between C\nand Rust.\n\n`b18_randomized_pattern_subject_fuzz`: with `Rng::new(0xREGEXP_SEED)` (pick a fixed constant)\ngenerate 20000 random patterns from a metacharacter alphabet\n`a b c A B . ^ $ * + ? | ( ) [ ] { } , - \\ 1 2 : = ! d s w D S W n x u 0 9 space` of length\n1..12, and random subjects of length 0..20 from `a b c A B \\n space 1 2 .`; for each, compile\nwith a random cflags from the 4 valid combos and exec with a random eflags. Assert compile\noutcome (NULL-ness + error string) and exec result match. Skip nothing — invalid patterns are\nthe point.\n\n`err_regexp_die_messages`: a table of `(pattern, cflags, expected_error_substring)` covering\nEVERY distinct `die()` message in regexp.c. Use exactly these triggers (verified against the C):\n```\n(\"a\\\\\",        0, \"unterminated escape sequence\")   // regexp.c:128 lone trailing backslash\n(\"a\\\\c\",       0, \"unterminated escape sequence\")   // :138\n(\"a\\\\x\",       0, \"unterminated escape sequence\")   // :143\n(\"a\\\\u12\",     0, \"unterminated escape sequence\")   // :153\n(\"\\\\xZZ\",      0, \"invalid escape sequence\")        // :101\n(\"\\\\uZZZZ\",    0, \"invalid escape sequence\")        // :101\n(\"a{x}\",       0, \"invalid quantifier\")             // :108\n(\"a{2,1}\",     0, \"invalid quantifier\")             // :598\n(\"a\\\\q\",       0, \"invalid escape character\")       // :170\n(\"a{255}\",     0, \"numeric overflow\")               // :186\n(\"a{1,255}\",   0, \"numeric overflow\")               // :200\n(\"[z-a]\",      0, \"invalid character class range\")  // :224\n(\"[a\",         0, \"unterminated character class\")   // :322\n(\"()*\",        0, \"infinite loop matching the empty string\") // :493\n(\"(a*)*\",      0, \"infinite loop matching the empty string\")\n(\"\\\\1\",        0, \"invalid back-reference\")         // :541\n(\"(\",          0, \"unmatched '('\")                  // :557\n(\"(?:\",        0, \"unmatched '('\")                  // :563\n(\"(?=\",        0, \"unmatched '('\")                  // :570\n(\"(?!\",        0, \"unmatched '('\")                  // :577\n(\"*\",          0, \"syntax error\")                   // :580\n(\"+\",          0, \"syntax error\")\n(\"(?\",         0, \"syntax error\")\n(\")\",          0, \"unmatched ')'\")                  // :940\n```\nplus these programmatically built ones:\n```\n\"[a]\".repeat(129)            -> \"too many character classes\"        // :213\n\"[\" + 32 chars \\x01,\\x03..\\x3F + \"]\" -> \"too many character class ranges\" // :253\n\"()\".repeat(16)              -> \"too many captures\"                 // :552\n\"a\".repeat(4097)             -> \"stack overflow\"                    // :661\n\"(?:a{254}){254}\"            -> \"program too large\"                 // :672\n\"a\".repeat(16385)            -> \"program too large\"                 // :922\n\"(?:a{254}){129}\"            -> \"program too large\"                 // :951\n```\nFor EACH row assert: BOTH libraries return NULL, and BOTH set `*errorp` to the SAME string,\nand that string contains the expected substring. ALSO assert the near-miss \"just inside the\nlimit\" variants compile successfully in BOTH (`\"[a]\".repeat(128)`, `\"()\".repeat(15)`,\n`\"a\".repeat(4096)`, `\"a{254}\"`, `\"a{1,254}\"`, `\"(?:a{254}){128}\"`, 31 spaced class chars).\n\n`err_regexec_exec_recursion_limit`: compile `\"a*\"` and exec against subjects of\n4000, 4094, 4095, 4096, 5000 `a` characters; assert the return value matches between C and\nRust (expect 0 for small, -1 past the REG_MAXREC=4096 execution-depth limit).\n\n`err_allocation_failure_paths`: using `js_regcompx` with an allocator that returns NULL on its\nNth non-zero-size call, drive the four \"cannot allocate ...\" die sites:\nN=1 with pattern \"a\" -> \"cannot allocate regular expression\";\nN=2 with pattern \"a\" -> \"cannot allocate regular expression parse list\";\nN=3 with pattern \"a\" -> \"cannot allocate regular expression instruction list\";\nN=4 with pattern \"[a]\" -> \"cannot allocate regular expression character class list\".\nAssert both libraries return NULL with the SAME error string for each N. Use a\n`static mut` counter reset before each call (single-threaded within the test).\n\n## Requirements\n- Free every `Reprog` you allocate (`js_regfree` / `js_regfreex`) to keep the test leak-free.\n- Every assert message must include the pattern, cflags, eflags and subject so a failure is\n  diagnosable.\n- The file must compile with `cargo test --no-run --test regexp`. RUN THAT COMMAND and fix all\n  errors before you finish. (First run `cargo build` so the cdylib exists.)\n- Then RUN `cargo test --test regexp 2>&1 | tail -60` and report the results verbatim.\n- DO NOT change the Rust library source (`src/*.rs`) even if a test fails — just report the\n  failure precisely. Your job is only to produce the test file.\n\nReport at the end: the exact number of `#[test]` functions, the pass/fail count, and the\nverbatim assertion message of every failure (truncated to 40 lines total).","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":true}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_c