-> Option<Vec<u8>>`,\n     `Rng::new(seed)` (`next_u64`, `next_u32`, `below`, `range`, `f64_bits`, `nice_f64`,\n     `pick`), `dbg_f64(f64) -> String`.\n   - `tests/utf.rs` is a working example of the style — read it.\n2. The C ground truth: `c_src/src/jsdtoa.c` (js_strtod, js_grisu2, js_fmtexp) and\n   `c_src/src/jsvalue.c` (js_itoa, js_strtol, js_stringtofloat, jsV_numbertostring,\n   jsV_stringtonumber, jsV_numbertoint32 etc.). READ THEM to learn the exact buffer sizes\n   each function requires and the meaning of the out-parameters.\n\n## Critical correctness rules for the test\n- Compare `f64` results by their RAW BITS (`f64::to_bits`), never with `==`, so NaN and −0.0\n  divergences are caught. Report with `dbg_f64`.\n- For functions taking an output buffer, allocate a generously oversized buffer\n  (e.g. 512 bytes) pre-filled with a poison byte (0xAA), and compare the WHOLE buffer\n  contents (not just up to the NUL) so stray writes are detected. Also compare the returned\n  pointer as an OFFSET from the buffer start (`ret as usize - buf.as_ptr() as usize`), never\n  as an absolute address.\n- For `end`/`endptr` out-parameters compare the OFFSET from the input string start.\n- `jsV_numbertostring` and `jsV_stringtonumber` need a `State`: create one per closure\n  invocation with `js_newstate(None, null_mut(), 0)` and free it at the end.\n  `jsV_numbertostring` returns a pointer that may or may not be into the supplied buffer —\n  compare the STRING CONTENTS, and separately compare the boolean\n  \"did it return a pointer inside the buffer\".\n\n## Tests to write (one `#[test]` per row of CONFIGS.md section C)\n\n`c1_strtod_corpus`: a table of ~150 hand-written input strings: \"0\", \"-0\", \"1\", \"1.5\",\n\"  1.5  \", \"+1.5\", \".5\", \"5.\", \"1e10\", \"1E+10\", \"1e-10\", \"1e\", \"1e+\", \"e10\", \"0x10\",\n\"0X1p3\", \"inf\", \"Inf\", \"INFINITY\", \"-infinity\", \"nan\", \"NaN\", \"-nan\", \"\", \" \", \"abc\",\n\"1abc\", \"0.0000000000000000000001\", \"1e308\", \"1e309\", \"1e-308\", \"1e-324\", \"1e-400\",\n\"179769313486231570000...\", 400-digit and 1000-digit mantissas, \"0000000000001\",\n\"1.7976931348623157e308\", \"2.2250738585072014e-308\", \"4.9406564584124654e-324\",\nstrings with leading tabs/newlines, \"--1\", \"1..2\", \"1e1e1\", very long digit strings, etc.\nCompare the returned double bits AND the `end` offset.\n\n`c2_strtod_overflow_underflow_and_maxexponent`: inputs exercising the `maxExponent` (511)\ntable path, `expSign`, and the >999-digit truncation logic in jsdtoa.c. Read the C to see\nwhich branches exist and craft an input for each (e.g. exponent exactly 511, 512, 1000,\nhuge negative exponents, mantissa longer than the internal buffer).\n\n`c3_grisu2_random_and_special`: call `js_grisu2(value, buf, &mut k)` for\n(a) all 8 special values ±0, ±inf, ±NaN, ±min-subnormal,\n(b) every power of two from 2^-1074 to 2^1023,\n(c) 200000 random `f64::from_bits(rng.next_u64())` values.\nCompare the returned length, the `k` out-parameter, and the whole buffer.\nNOTE: read jsdtoa.c to check whether js_grisu2 is defined for inf/nan/0 — if the C would\nread uninitialised memory or loop forever for some class of input, EXCLUDE that class and\nsay so in a comment; do not crash the test.\n\n`c4_fmtexp_all_exponents`: `js_fmtexp(buf, e)` for every e in -400..400 plus i32::MIN,\ni32::MAX. Compare buffer and returned offset.\n\n`c5_itoa_all_radices`: `js_itoa(buf, v, radix)` for radix 2..36 (and also invalid radices\n0, 1, 37, -1 — read the C to see what it does; if it would loop forever or write out of\nbounds, exclude and comment) crossed with v in\n{i32::MIN, i32::MIN+1, -1000000, -1, 0, 1, 10, 35, 36, 1000000, i32::MAX} plus 2000 random\ni32 values. Compare buffer + returned offset.\n\n`c6_strtol_all_bases`: `js_strtol(s, &mut end, base)` for base in 0..=36 and -1, 37 crossed\nwith ~80 input strings (signs, prefixes \"0x\"/\"0X\"/\"0\", whitespace, overflow like\n\"99999999999999999999999\", \"-99999999999999999999999\", junk, empty). Compare the returned\ndouble bits and the `end` offset.\n\n`c7_numbertostring_random_and_boundaries`: `jsV_numbertostring(J, buf, v)` for the\nboundary values 0, -0, 1, -1, NaN, ±inf, 1e20, 1e21, 1e-6, 1e-7, 0.1, 1/3, 2^53, 2^53+2,\ni32::MIN as f64, 1e300, 5e-324, and 200000 random `nice_f64()` values. Compare the string\ncontents, the whole buffer, and whether the returned pointer was inside the buffer.\n\n`c8_stringtonumber_corpus`: `jsV_stringtonumber(J, s)` over the same ~150 strings as c1 plus\nJS-specific forms: \"\", \" \", \"\\t\\n\", \"0x1f\", \"0X1F\", \"0b11\", \"0o17\", \"017\", \"Infinity\",\n\"-Infinity\", \"+Infinity\", \"1_000\", \"1e\", \".\", \"-.\", \"1.\", \".1\", \"  12  \", \"12abc\".\nCompare raw bits.\n\n`c9_stringtofloat_corpus`: same corpus as c8 via `js_stringtofloat(s, &mut end)`; compare\nbits + end offset.\n\n`c10_number_coercions_exhaustive`: for `jsV_numbertointeger`, `jsV_numbertoint32`,\n`jsV_numbertouint32`, `jsV_numbertoint16`, `jsV_numbertouint16`: the boundary set\n{0, -0, NaN, ±inf, 0.5, -0.5, 1.5, -1.5, 2^31, -2^31, 2^31-1, 2^32, 2^32-1, 2^32+1, 2^53,\n-2^53, 2^63, 1e300, -1e300, 65535, 65536, 32767, -32768, 32768, i32::MIN as f64} plus\n300000 random values from `f64_bits()` and `nice_f64()`. Compare exact results.\n\n## Requirements\n- Free every state you create.\n- Every assertion message must print the input (with `{:?}` for strings and `dbg_f64` for\n  doubles) so a failure is diagnosable.\n- The file must compile: run `cargo build` then `cargo test --no-run --test numbers` and fix\n  all compile errors.\n- Then run `cargo test --test numbers 2>&1 | tail -60` and report results verbatim.\n- DO NOT change the Rust library source (`src/*.rs`) even if a test fails — just report the\n  failure precisely.\n\nReport at the end: the number of `#[test]` functions, the pass/fail count, and the verbatim\nassertion message of every distinct failure (truncated to 40 lines total).","uuid":"53843c54-d1af-4615-b930-4e10cfc88c04","session_id":"f3ff8386-8592-4dbd-8b83-c0778c2e2173"}
