e.c:336` — `propname()` can only produce `EXP_NUMBER`/`EXP_STRING`/`AST_IDENTIFIER`, all handled above; constant folding never rewrites a property-name node.\n- `jscompile.c:487` (`cassignop2`) — always preceded by `cassignop1` on the same lhs with an identical case set, so jscompile.c:464 fires first.\n- `jscompile.c:780` (`cexp` default) — purely defensive; `EXP_PROP_*` are consumed by `cobject`, `EXP_VAR` by `cvarinit`/`cvardecs`, `STM_CASE`/`STM_DEFAULT` by `cswitch`, `AST_LIST` by `cstmlist`. I fuzzed 20 odd constructs (getters/setters, numeric and string keys, `for(var a=1 in b)`, nested labels, elisions, etc.) and none reached it.\n\n**3 sites are reachable but not with a compact snippet** (marked `HARD:`, all three confirmed to actually fire):\n- `jscompile.c:75` — `emit()` writes `F->lastline` first, so a source with 65536 newlines overflows `js_Instruction` (`unsigned short`). Verified: `SyntaxError: integer overflow in instruction coding`.\n- `jscompile.c:238` vs `245` — both say \"jump address integer overflow\" but are distinguishable: `\"1;\"×15000` then `while(0);` hits `emitjumpto` (238, backward jump target), while `\"1;\"×15000` then `if(0);` hits `labelto` (245, forward-jump patch) with no `emitjumpto` in that path. Both verified.\n\n**Two surprises worth flagging for the Rust port:**\n- Two sites report **line 0**, not line 1: `jscompile.c:315` (`SyntaxError: [string]:0: duplicate property 'a' in object literal`) and `jscompile.c:410` (`...:0: more than one loop variable in for-in statement`). Both pass an `AST_LIST` node to `jsC_error`, and `LIST(h)` is constructed with a hardcoded `line` of 0.\n- Three different **prefix conventions** coexist: `jsY_error`/`jsP_error`/`jsC_error` → `SyntaxError: [string]:<line>: msg`; `js_syntaxerror` in jscompile.c → bare `SyntaxError: msg` (no prefix at all); json.c's `js_syntaxerror` → `SyntaxError: JSON: msg` (the \"JSON:\" is part of the format string, not a filename); and json lexer errors → `SyntaxError: 