{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_faswmsrb4p6yqduwxfigtukkrepc4uu7jcr4uinhkkur2jaisxuq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_019WMd9TjfscpAoEgBRzf7iE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write script corpus tests part 1","prompt":"Working directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hWAEAP/translated_rust\n\nTask: write ONE new file `tests/scripts_core.rs` — a large differential JavaScript corpus for\nthe MuJS interpreter. Do NOT modify any other file (not `c_src/`, not `tests/common/mod.rs`,\nnot `src/*.rs`, not `CONFIGS.md`/`ERRORS.md`/`SYMBOLS.md`, not other `tests/*.rs`).\n\n## Harness (read `tests/common/mod.rs` and `tests/smoke.rs` first)\n```rust\nmod common;\nuse common::*;\n\n// runs the script through js_dostring on a fresh state in BOTH libraries and\n// asserts (return code, captured output) are byte-identical.\ndiff_scripts(flags, &[\"script1\", \"script2\", ...]);\ndiff_scripts_both_modes(&[...]);   // runs with flags=0 and flags=JS_STRICT\n```\nA global `print(...)` function is pre-installed; it joins its arguments with a space and\nappends \"\\n\" to the captured output. Uncaught errors are captured as\n`[report] TypeError: ...`. So EVERY script should `print(...)` the values it computes —\na script that computes without printing proves almost nothing.\n\n## Hard rules for the corpus\n1. **NO nondeterminism.** Never use `Math.random()`, `Date.now()`, `new Date()` with no\n   arguments, or anything that reads the clock. (A separate test file covers those.)\n2. Prefer many SMALL scripts over few big ones: when one diverges you want to know exactly\n   which construct broke. Aim for **at least 450 scripts total** across the tests below.\n3. Print results in a way that exposes representation: e.g.\n   `print(x, typeof x, JSON.stringify(x))`.\n4. Wrap deliberately-throwing snippets in `try{...}catch(e){print('caught', e.name, e.message)}`\n   in the *valid-path* tests, so the test compares the caught error rather than aborting the\n   script — but ALSO include a few unwrapped ones so the top-level report path is compared.\n5. Every string literal in Rust that contains a backslash must be written as a raw string\n   (`r\"...\"` / `r#\"...\"#`) or properly escaped. Be careful: JS regexps and `\\n` escapes are\n   the main hazard. Prefer `r#\"...\"#`.\n\n## Tests to write (these are CONFIGS.md rows H1..H10)\n\n`h1_operators`: every operator. Arithmetic (`+ - * / %` incl. `-0`, `NaN`, `Infinity`,\nstring+number coercions, `\"a\"+1`, `1+\"a\"`, `[]+{}`, `{}+[]`), bitwise (`& | ^ ~`) on values\naround 2^31/2^32 and NaN/Infinity, shifts (`<< >> >>>`) with counts 0,1,31,32,33,-1,\ncomparison (`< > <= >=` on numbers, strings, mixed, NaN), equality (`== != === !==` across\nall type pairs incl. null/undefined/NaN/-0/objects/boxed primitives), logical (`&& || !`\nshort-circuiting with side effects), `typeof` on every value kind, `void`, `delete` (on\nproperties, array elements, unresolvable names, non-configurable), `in`, `instanceof`,\ncomma, ternary, prefix/postfix `++`/`--` on variables, properties and array elements,\ncompound assignments (`+= -= *= /= %= <<= >>= >>>= &= |= ^=`), unary `+`/`-` on strings and\nobjects. Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h2_statements`: `var` (with and without initialiser, multiple declarators, hoisting),\n`if/else` chains, `do/while`, `while`, `for` (all three clauses empty/present), `for-in`\n(over object, array, string object, with prototype chain, with DONTENUM), `for(var x in ...)`,\n`switch` (with/without `default`, `default` in the middle, fallthrough, string and object\ncases, empty body), `break`/`continue` labelled and unlabelled at several nesting depths,\nnested labels, `return` (bare and with value, inside try/finally), `throw`,\n`try/catch`, `try/finally`, `try/catch/finally` (including `return`/`break`/`continue`\ncrossing a `finally`, and a `throw` inside `finally` overriding), `with` (non-strict only),\n`debugger`, empty statement, block statements, automatic semicolon insertion cases.\nUse `diff_scripts_both_modes` for the strict-compatible ones and `diff_scripts(0, ...)`\nfor the `with` ones.\n\n`h3_functions`: function declarations vs expressions vs named function expressions,\nhoisting order, closures and captured loop variables, recursion (factorial, fibonacci,\nmutual recursion), `arguments` (indexing, `.length`, `.callee`, aliasing of parameters,\nwriting through `arguments[0]`), default-missing parameters (`undefined`), extra arguments,\n`fn.length`, `fn.name` if supported (check the C first — do not assume), nested function\nscopes, IIFEs, functions returning functions, `new` on a user function (with and without an\nexplicit return of an object), `this` inside methods vs plain calls, constructor `prototype`\nchains, `Function.prototype.constructor`. Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h4_object_builtins`: exercise EVERY function in `c_src/src/jsobject.c` — read that file and\nenumerate them. For each: valid uses on plain objects/arrays/functions, property descriptors\nwith every combination of `value`/`writable`/`enumerable`/`configurable`/`get`/`set`,\n`Object.create` with a null prototype and with a properties argument, `seal`/`freeze`/\n`preventExtensions` followed by attempted mutation (both sloppy — silent — and strict —\nthrows), `hasOwnProperty`/`isPrototypeOf`/`propertyIsEnumerable`/`toString`/`toLocaleString`/\n`valueOf` on many receivers, `Object()` called as a function and with `new` on every\nprimitive. Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h5_array_builtins`: read `c_src/src/jsarray.c` and enumerate EVERY prototype method. For\neach method test: empty array, one element, many elements, array with holes\n(`[1,,3]`, `delete a[1]`), array-like plain object receiver (`{length:n, ...}`), string\nreceiver via `.call`, negative and out-of-range index/start/end arguments, `undefined`\narguments. Plus `Array()`, `Array(5)`, `Array(1,2,3)`, `Array.isArray` on many values,\n`length` assignment (grow, shrink to 0, non-integer, huge), and the flat/sparse transition\n(assign index 0..5 then index 1000, then read back and enumerate).\nUse `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h6_array_sort`: `sort` with no comparator (string comparison, `undefined` sorting last),\nwith a numeric comparator, with an inconsistent comparator, with a comparator that throws,\non 0/1/2/3/17/200 elements (crossing MuJS's heapsort threshold — read `Ap_sort` in jsarray.c\nto find it and cover both sides), on arrays with holes and `undefined`s, on an array-like\nobject, and `reverse` on the same shapes. Also `sort` where the comparator mutates the array.\nUse `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h7_string_builtins`: read `c_src/src/jsstring.c` and enumerate EVERY prototype method plus\n`String.fromCharCode`. Test each against: `\"\"`, `\"a\"`, ASCII, strings with multi-byte UTF-8\n(`\"héllo\"`, `\"日本語\"`, `\"\\u{1F600}\"` written as a surrogate pair escape), strings of length\n15/16/17 (the shrstr boundary), and with negative / fractional / out-of-range / `undefined`\nindex arguments. `split` with a string separator, an empty separator, a regexp separator\n(with and without capture groups) and every limit 0..5 and `undefined`. `replace` with a\nstring replacement containing `$$ $& $` $' $1 $9 $10`, with a function replacement (check the\narguments it receives), and with a `g` and non-`g` regexp. `match`/`search` with and without\n`g`. `charAt`/`charCodeAt`/`indexOf`/`lastIndexOf`/`slice`/`substring`/`substr`/`concat`/\n`toUpperCase`/`toLowerCase`/`trim` etc. Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h8_number_builtins`: `(v).toString(radix)` for radix 2..36 and v in a wide set (integers,\nnegatives, fractions, 0, -0, NaN, Infinity, 1e21, 1e-7, 2^53); `toFixed(0..20)`,\n`toExponential(0..20)`, `toPrecision(1..21)` on the same values; `Number()` with no args, with\nevery primitive, with objects; `Number.MAX_VALUE/MIN_VALUE/NaN/POSITIVE_INFINITY/\nNEGATIVE_INFINITY`; `parseInt`/`parseFloat` are covered elsewhere. Also `String(v)` and\nimplicit `\"\"+v` for the same value set (this exercises jsV_numbertostring).\nUse `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h9_math_builtins`: read `c_src/src/jsmath.c` and enumerate every function EXCEPT `random`.\nFor each, call with 0 args, 1 arg over\n`[-0, 0, 0.5, -0.5, 1, -1, 2, -2, 0.1, 1e300, -1e300, 1e-300, NaN, Infinity, -Infinity,\nMath.PI, Math.E, 1e21, -1e21]`, 2 args (for `atan2`, `pow`), and 0/1/3/5 args for `max`/`min`\n(including NaN and -0 vs 0). Print with full precision (e.g. `print(x.toString(), 1/x)` so\n`-0` is visible). Also all `Math` constants. Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n`h10_boolean_and_function_methods`: `Boolean()` / `new Boolean()` on every primitive,\n`Boolean.prototype.toString/valueOf` on correct and wrong receivers (wrapped in try/catch);\n`Function.prototype.call`/`apply` with `null`/`undefined`/primitive/object `this`, with an\narray/arguments-object/array-like/`undefined` argument list, with too many arguments;\n`Function.prototype.bind` (partial application, `length` of the bound function, using the\nbound function with `new`, binding a bound function, `bind` on a cfunction like\n`Math.max`); `Function.prototype.toString` on user functions, built-ins and bound functions;\n`new Function(\"a\",\"b\",\"return a+b\")` and `Function()` with a syntax error in the body\n(caught). Use `diff_scripts_both_modes`.\n\n## Requirements\n- Run `cargo build` first (the cdylib must exist), then\n  `cargo test --no-run --test scripts_core` and fix all compile errors.\n- Then run `cargo test --test scripts_core 2>&1 | tail -80`.\n- **If a script diverges, DO NOT change the Rust source and DO NOT delete or weaken the\n  script.** Keep it and report it. The divergences are the whole point — they are bugs in the\n  Rust translation that will be fixed separately.\n- If a script makes the test process CRASH (segfault/abort) rather than just diverge, move\n  that script into a clearly marked `// CRASHES:` comment list at the bottom of the file and\n  report it separately, so the rest of the suite can run.\n\nReport at the end: the number of `#[test]` functions, the total number of scripts in the\ncorpus (count them), the pass/fail count, and for every failing test the FULL verbatim list\nof diverging scripts with their C and Rust outputs (up to 100 lines total).","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":true}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5773,"cache_read_input_tokens":160373,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5773,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3ff8386-8592-4dbd-8b83-c0778c2e2173","uuid":"7de34307-d619-44bb-ba64-b5232979ff8b","timestamp":"2026-08-14T19:39:23.938Z"}
