lements (including `undefined` for unmatched optional groups); `String.prototype.match`\nwith and without `g`; `search`; `split` with a regexp; `replace` with a regexp and a\nfunction; regexps against multi-byte UTF-8 subjects; anchors with and without `m`;\nback-references; lookahead; character classes with `\\d \\w \\s` and their negations;\n`toString` of a regexp; invalid patterns and invalid/duplicate flags (caught).\n\n`h12_date_deterministic`: read `c_src/src/jsdate.c`. Cover WITHOUT reading the clock:\n`new Date(ms)` for many fixed ms values (0, negative, 1e12, ±8.64e15 = the max time value,\n8.64e15+1 = invalid, NaN); `new Date(y,m[,d,h,mi,s,ms])` with 2..7 arguments including\nout-of-range components that roll over (month 13, day 0, day 32, hour 24, negative values);\n`new Date(string)` and `Date.parse(string)` over many fixed format strings (ISO with and\nwithout timezone, `\"2020-01-01\"`, `\"2020-01-01T00:00:00Z\"`, `\"2020-01-01T00:00:00.123+05:30\"`,\nRFC-style `\"Mon Jan 01 2020\"`, garbage, empty); `Date.UTC` with 1..7 arguments; every getter\n(`getTime getFullYear getMonth getDate getDay getHours getMinutes getSeconds\ngetMilliseconds getTimezoneOffset` and all the `getUTC*` variants) on several fixed\ntimestamps including an invalid date; every setter (`setTime setFullYear setMonth setDate\nsetHours setMinutes setSeconds setMilliseconds` and the `setUTC*` variants) with in-range,\nout-of-range and `NaN` arguments; `toString`, `toDateString`, `toTimeString`, `toUTCString`,\n`toISOString` (incl. the RangeError for an invalid date, caught), `toJSON`, `valueOf`,\n`toLocaleString`/`toLocaleDateString`/`toLocaleTimeString` if present. Also\n`Date.prototype.<method>.call(nonDate)` for a few methods (caught).\nNOTE: results depend on the `TZ` environment variable, but BOTH libraries run in the SAME\nprocess so they see the same `TZ` — that is fine and is exactly what we want to compare.\nAdd a SECOND test `h12b_date_clock_dependent` that (a) calls `Date.now()`/`new Date()` in\nboth libraries and only asserts both produce a number within 2000 ms of each other and of\nthe Rust `SystemTime` clock, and (b) does NOT compare exact output. Explain in a comment why.\n\n`h13_json`: read `c_src/src/json.c`. `JSON.parse` on: every value shape (null, true, false,\nnumbers incl. negative/exponent/fraction, strings with every escape `\\\" \\\\ \\/ \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t\n\\uXXXX` and multi-byte UTF-8, arrays, objects, deep nesting, empty array/object, duplicate\nkeys), with and without a reviver (a reviver that returns `undefined` to delete, one that\ntransforms, one that throws), and on malformed input (caught). `JSON.stringify` on: every\nvalue shape, `undefined`/functions (omitted vs `null` in arrays), `NaN`/`Infinity`, `-0`,\nnested objects/arrays, objects with a `toJSON` method, `Date` objects (use a fixed timestamp),\nboxed primitives, cyclic structures (caught), with a replacer FUNCTION, with a replacer ARRAY\n(including one containing numbers and duplicates), and with `space` = `undefined`, 0, 1, 4,\n10, 11, `\"  \"`, `\"\\t\"`, a 12-character string, and a non-string/non-number.\n`JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` round trips.\n\n`h14_errors`: all seven constructors (`Error EvalError RangeError ReferenceError SyntaxError\nTypeError URIError`) called with `new` and without, with no message / a string message / an\nobject message; `name`, `message`, `toString()`; overriding `name` and `message` (including\nsetting them to `\"\"`); `instanceof`; `e.stack` and `e.stackTrace` (print only\n`typeof e.stack` and whether it contains \"at\", NOT the full trace, since it embeds line\nnumbers that must still match — actually DO print the full `e.stack` too, it must match\nbyte-for-byte); throwing non-Error values (numbers, strings, `null`, `undefined`, objects,\narrays) and catching them; rethrowing; nested try/catch; an error thrown from a getter, from\na `toString`, from inside `JSON.stringify`, from inside `Array.prototype.map`; the\n`Error.prototype.toString` edge cases where `name` or `message` is empty.\n\n`h15_global_functions`: read `c_src/src/jsbuiltin.c`. `parseInt` with every radix 0..37 and\n-1 crossed with many strings (signs, `0x` prefixes, whitespace, partial digits, empty,\nhuge); `parseFloat` on many strings; `isNaN`/`isFinite` on every value kind;\n`encodeURI`/`encodeURIComponent`/`decodeURI`/`decodeURIComponent` on ASCII, the reserved and\nunreserved sets, multi-byte UTF-8, lone surrogates, and malformed `%` escapes (caught);\n`escape`/`unescape` if present; `eval` (as a function and indirectly, returning values,\ndeclaring variables, in strict and sloppy mode, with a syntax error caught, nested eval);\n`Function` constructor; the global object's own properties (`NaN`, `Infinity`, `undefined`)\nand attempts to overwrite them.\n\n`h16_enumeration_and_prototypes`: `for-in` enumeration ORDER over objects built in various\nways (literal, incremental assignment, after deletes, with numeric-looking keys, mixed with\narray indices), shadowing over a prototype chain (2 and 3 levels deep), non-enumerable\nproperties created with `defineProperty`, enumeration of arrays with holes, of string\nobjects, of functions, of `arguments`; deleting a property during `for-in`; adding a property\nduring `for-in`; `Object.keys` vs `for-in` vs `Object.getOwnPropertyNames` on the same\nobjects.\n\n`h17_lexing`: number literals (decimal, `.5`, `5.`, `1e10`, `1E-10`, `0x1f`, `0X1F`, huge,\ntiny), string literals with every escape (`\\' \\\" \\\\ \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t \\v \\0 \\xHH \\uHHHH`, a\nline continuation, an unknown escape like `\\q` — note `\\q` at statement level is a syntax\nerror but inside a string it is just `q`), single vs double quotes, strings containing\nmulti-byte UTF-8; regexp literals vs division disambiguation (`a/b/c`, `a=/b/g`,\n`(1)/2/3`); comments (`//`, `/* */`, a `/* */` containing a newline, a comment at EOF);\nline terminators `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\r\\n`, `\u2028`, `\u2029` and their effect on automatic\nsemicolon insertion; identifiers with `$`, `_`, digits, and non-ASCII letters; all keywords\nused as property names (`o.if`, `o[\"class\"]`, `({if:1}).if`); `\\u`-escaped identifiers if\nsupported (check the C first).\n\n`h18_stress`: 100-deep nested object literal; 100-deep nested array literal; a 1000-element\narray literal; a 2000-character string literal; a function with 100 parameters; a function\nwith 200 local variables; 300-deep nested parentheses (just under the JS_ASTLIMIT of 400 —\nread `jsparse.c` INCREC/JS_ASTLIMIT and cover both just-under and just-over, the latter\nproducing a caught/reported SyntaxError); an `if/else if` chain 200 long; a `switch` with 300\ncases; recursion 500 deep and recursion deep enough to overflow (report must match); a loop\nrunning 100000 iterations accumulating a value; string concatenation in a loop building a\n64KB string (print only its length and a checksum, not the string).\n\n`h19_this_and_arguments`: `this` at top level, in a plain function call, in a method call, in\na constructor, in a function called with `call/apply/bind` passing `null`/`undefined`/a\nnumber/a string/an object — in BOTH strict and sloppy mode (the boxing behaviour differs, so\nthis is important). `arguments` aliasing of named parameters in sloppy vs strict mode,\n`arguments.callee`, `arguments.length` vs `fn.length`, modifying `arguments` and reading the\nparameter, and vice versa.\n\n`h20_expression_fuzz`: with `Rng::new(0x5EED_1234)` from the harness, GENERATE ~4000 random\nJavaScript expressions from a small grammar (numeric and string literals, variables bound to\na fixed set of values, the binary operators `+ - * / % & | ^ << >> >>> < > <= >= == != ===\n!== && ||`, unary `- + ~ !`, parentheses, `?:`, method calls like `.toString()`,\n`.charAt(1)`, `Math.floor(...)`) of bounded depth, wrap each as\n`try{print(EXPR)}catch(e){print('E',e.name)}`, and diff them. Build the script strings in\nRust and pass them to `diff_scripts`. Use a fixed seed so failures are reproducible, and\nprint the generated expression in the failure message (`diff_scripts` already does this).\n\n## Requirements\n- Run `cargo build` first, then `cargo test --no-run --test scripts_lib`, fix all compile\n  errors (raw strings are the usual problem).\n- Then run `cargo test --test scripts_lib 2>&1 | tail -80`.\n- **If a script diverges, DO NOT change the Rust source and DO NOT delete or weaken the\n  script.** Keep it and report it verbatim.\n- If a script CRASHES the process (segfault/abort), move it to a `// CRASHES:` comment list\n  at the bottom and report it separately so the rest can run.\n\nReport at the end: the number of `#[test]` functions, the total script count, the pass/fail\ncount, and for every failing test the FULL verbatim list of diverging scripts with their C\nand Rust outputs (up to 100 lines total).","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hWAEAP/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-hWAEAP-translated-rust/f3ff8386-8592-4dbd-8b83-c0778c2e2173/tasks/a56a4371939942347.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
