json.c` embeds the literal `\"JSON: \"` in its format strings, while the\n  JSON *lexer* really does use `\"JSON\"` as the filename (`SyntaxError: JSON:1: ...`).\n* `regexp.c`'s `die()` makes `regcompx` return `NULL` and set `*errorp` to the\n  message. The test compares NULL-ness **and** the error string.\n\nLegend for the \"reachable\" column: `JS` = a `js_dostring` snippet, `JS/strict` =\nneeds `\"use strict\"`, `CAPI` = a direct C-API call sequence, `NO` = provably\nunreachable (justified in the trigger column), `HARD` = reachable but needs a\ngenerated multi-kilobyte source.\n\nTest files: `tests/errors_*.rs` (Phase C).\n\n\"\"\")\n\ndef emit(title, rows, start, note=\"\"):\n    out.append(\"## %s\\n\\n\" % title)\n    if note: out.append(note+\"\\n\\n\")\n    out.append(\"| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | reachable | [x] |\\n\")\n    out.append(\"|---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|-----------|-----|\\n\")\n    n=start\n    for r in rows:\n        out.append(\"| E%d | `%s` (%s) | %s | %s | %s | %s |\\n\" % (n, r['fn'], r['loc'], r['trig'], r['exp'], r['reach'], r['mark']))\n        n+=1\n    out.append(\"\\n\")\n    return n\n\ndef load(path, kind):\n    rows=[]\n    for line in open(path):\n        f=line.rstrip(\"\\n\").split(\"\\t\")\n        rows.append(f)\n    return rows\n\ndef mkrow(loc,fn,msg,exp,mode,trig,kind):\n    reach='JS'\n    mark='[x]'\n    t=trig\n    if fn.startswith('(declaration)') or fn=='(definition)':\n        reach='n/a'; mark='n/a'\n        return dict(loc=loc,fn=fn,trig='not a rejection — this is the declaration/definition of the error helper',exp='—',reach=reach,mark=mark)\n    if t.startswith('UNREACHABLE'):\n        reach='NO'; mark='[x] (proved unreachable)'\n    elif t.startswith('HARD'):\n        reach='HARD'\n    elif mode=='yes':\n        reach='JS/strict'\n    elif kind=='CAPI' or mode=='CAPI':\n        reach='CAPI'\n    t=t.replace('|','\\\\|').replace('UNREACHABLE:','**unreachable** — ').replace('HARD:','**hard** — ')\n    return dict(loc=loc,fn=fn,trig='`%s`'%t if not t.startswith('**') else t,\n                exp='`%s`'%exp.replace('|','\\\\|') if exp not in ('N/A','—') else '—',\n                reach=reach,mark=mark)\n\nn=1\n# group 1\nrows=[]\nfor f in load('/tmp/triggers1.tsv','JS'):\n    rows.append(mkrow(f[0],f[1],f[2],f[3],f[4],f[5],'JS'))\nn=emit(\"Section 1 — `jsarray.c` `jsboolean.c` `jsbuiltin.c` `jsdate.c` `jserror.c` `jsfunction.c` `jsnumber.c` `jsobject.c`\", rows, n,\n       \"All rows verified by executing the trigger against the C library and comparing the report string.\")\nopen('/tmp/errors_part1.md','w').writelines(out)\nprint(\"part1 rows through E%d\"%(n-1))\nPY\nhead -40 /tmp/errors_part1.md","description":"Generate ERRORS.md part 1"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":157,"cache_read_input_tokens":179366,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":157,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3ff8386-8592-4dbd-8b83-c0778c2e2173","uuid":"d56bcc24-d6e0-49b1-ae35-6900d7831c44","timestamp":"2026-08-14T19:41:21.968Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019rmgczjApQudVu3gEd53Q6","type":"tool_result","content":"part1 rows through E58\n# ERRORS.md — error-surface table (Phase A)\n\nDerived **mechanically** from the C source. The row set is the union of:\n\n1. every call site of an error-raising function in `c_src/src/*.c`, found with\n   ```\n   grep -nE 'js_(error|evalerror|rangeerror|referenceerror|syntaxerror|typeerror|urierror)\\(|jsP_error|jsC_error|jsY_error|die\\(' c_src/src/*.c\n   ```\n   → 225 hits, of which 7 are the declarations/definitions of the error helpers\n   themselves, leaving **218 distinct rejection branches**;\n2. every explicit resource/limit rejection (`js_stackoverflow`, `js_outofmemory`,\n   `js_runlimit`, `js_trystackoverflow`, `CHECKSTACK`, `INCREC`, `REG_MAX*`);\n3. the return-code contracts of the protected entry points (`js_dostring`,\n   `js_ploadstring`, `js_pcall`, `js_pconstruct`, `regcomp`, `regexec`);\n4. the generic FFI boundaries every C API has: NULL pointers, zero/oversized\n   lengths, one-past-range values, and **out-of-range enum values** (a C enum\n   parameter accepts any `int`).\n\nHow the error is observed in the differential test:\n* `js_*error()` raises a JS exception; `js_dostring` returns `1` and passes\n  `Error.prototype.toString()` (i.e. `\"<Name>: <message>\"`) to the report\n  callback. The test compares the return code **and** the exact report string.\n* `jsY_error`/`jsP_error`/`jsC_error` produce `SyntaxError: <file>:<line>: <msg>`.\n  Note the C builds this with `snprintf(buf, 256, \"%s:%d: \", ...)` followed by\n  `strcat(buf, msgbuf)` into a `char buf[512]` — the prefix is truncated at 256,\n  which the Rust must reproduce.\n* `js_syntaxerror` called directly from `jscompile.c` has **no** `file:line`\n  prefix. `json.c` embeds the literal `\"JSON: \"` in its format strings, while the\n  JSON *lexer* really does use `\"JSON\"` as the filename (`SyntaxError: JSON:1: ...`).\n* `reg