{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_2t2awjno4ugg5e6yxajz6bcwawhoj63bbkeajvdrxkeno7gw6u7a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Summary of the work:\n\n## What was written\n\nOne new file: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hWAEAP/translated_rust/tests/regexp.rs` (~880 lines). No other file was touched (verified by mtimes: `c_src/`, `src/`, `tests/common/mod.rs`, `CONFIGS.md`, `SYMBOLS.md` all unchanged).\n\n**Comparison discipline** — two helpers reduce everything to address-independent values, as required:\n- `compile_result` / `compile_result_x` → `(is_null, Option<error string>)`. `*errorp` is pre-poisoned with `0x1`, so \"library never wrote `*errorp`\" surfaces as the string `<*errorp WAS NOT WRITTEN>` instead of a segfault.\n- `exec_result(api, prog, subject, sub, eflags) -> (c_int, c_int, Vec<Option<(isize,isize,Vec<u8>)>>)` → `(ret, sub.nsub, 16 slots)`; each slot is `None` for NULL `sp`, else `(sp-base, ep-base, matched bytes)`, with `isize::MIN` / `<span outside subject>` sentinels for a NULL/out-of-range `ep`. Every `Reprog` is freed with `js_regfree`/`js_regfreex`.\n\n**10 `#[test]` functions**, exactly the requested names. Two of them (`err_regexp_die_messages`, `err_regexec_exec_recursion_limit`) run on a 256 MiB `std::thread` stack, because they deliberately drive the libraries' 4096-deep `count()`/`match()` recursion and the default 2 MiB test-thread stack is not enough head-room.\n\n## Two corrections I had to make to the task's spec (verified against the C first, via a throwaway dlopen driver)\n\n1. The suggested `\"too many character class ranges\"` trigger — `[` + `\\x01,\\x03..\\x3F` + `]` — **does not fail**: `0x2D` in that sequence is `-`, which `lexclass` treats as a range operator, so only 30 of the 32 spans materialize and the pattern compiles OK in C. Replaced with `spaced_class(n)`: `[` + runes `U+0100, U+0102, ...` (step 2, so `addrange` can never merge them, and no character is a class metacharacter). 32 runes → `too many character class ranges`; the 31-rune near-miss compiles. Confirmed against C.\n2. Everything else in the table was confirmed verbatim against the C library, including all near-misses (`[a]`×128, `()`×15, `a`×4096, `a{254}`, `a{1,254}`, `(?:a{254}){128}` all compile; `a`×16384 fails with `stack overflow`, not `program too large`, which is why only the 16385 variant is asserted).\n\n## Coverage actually exercised (measured with temporary instrumentation, since removed)\n\n- `b1_b6`: 75 patterns × 8 cflags = 600 compiles (all succeed) and **64,800 regexec calls per library**, 24,356 matches, 26,476 non-NULL capture slots compared.\n- `b18` fuzz (seed `0x5EED_C0FFEE_9E37`, 20,000 cases): 9,048 compiled / 10,952 rejected / 1,139 matched, hitting 12 distinct `die()` messages.\n- `err_regexec_exec_recursion_limit`: C and Rust agree exactly — `ret=0` at 4000 and 4094 `a`s, `ret=-1` at 4095, 4096, 5000 (REG_MAXREC boundary).\n\n## Results\n\n`cargo test --no-run --test regexp` compiles cleanly. `cargo test --test regexp 2>&1 | tail -60` (verbatim last lines; the preceding 58 lines are all repeats of the same two-line pair):\n\n```\nthread '<unnamed>' (1980779) panicked at src/regexp.rs:93:5:\nBox<dyn Any>\ntest b18_randomized_pattern_subject_fuzz ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 10 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.36s\n```\n\n- **`#[test]` functions: 10. Passed: 10. Failed: 0.** No assertion messages to report — zero divergences found between the C and Rust regexp engines.\n\nOne caveat for whoever reads this output next: the ~11,000 `panicked at src/regexp.rs:93 / Box<dyn Any>` stderr lines are **not** failures — they are the Rust translation's `die()`/`longjmp` emulation (a panic it catches itself inside `js_regcompx`), printed once per invalid pattern. They cannot be filtered from the test side (the cdylib statically links its own std and therefore has its own panic hook — I tried and reverted it), so use `cargo test --test regexp 2>&1 | grep -v \"Box<dyn Any>\"` for readable output. I verified with a temporarily injected failure that a real assertion message still lands in the trailing `failures:` section and so remains visible in `tail -60`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1159,"cache_read_input_tokens":145480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1159,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Fkf11ZHqqXH143gDdzweMU","session_id":"f3ff8386-8592-4dbd-8b83-c0778c2e2173","uuid":"36ba0d21-18a0-480b-9a53-e61838c58896","timestamp":"2026-08-14T19:50:02.652Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write regexp differential tests"}
